believe I found a bug in the joint value interpolation code for floating joints.\r\n\r\nSee the snippet below:\r\n\r\n```\r\nconst double jv1[] = {0,0,0, 0, 0, 0.862044, 0.506833};\r\nconst double jv2[] = { 0,0,0, 0, 0, -0.969825, 0.243803 };\r\n\r\ndouble intp[7];\r\n\r\nmoveit::core::FloatingJointModel fjm(\"joint\", 0, 0);\r\n\r\nfjm.interpolate(jv1, jv2, 0.5, intp);\r\n\r\nEigen::Isometry3d tf;\r\n\r\nfjm.computeTransform(jv1, tf);\r\nEigen::AngleAxisd rotA(tf.rotation());\r\n\r\nfjm.computeTransform(jv2, tf);\r\nEigen::AngleAxisd rotB(tf.rotation());\r\n\r\nfjm.computeTransform(intp, tf);\r\nEigen::AngleAxisd rotC(tf.rotation());\r\n\r\nstd::cout << \"AA: \" << rotA.angle() << \" \" << rotA.axis().transpose() << std::endl; // 2.07858 0 0 1\r\nstd::cout << \"BB: \" << rotB.angle() << \" \" << rotB.axis().transpose() << std::endl; // 2.64902 -0 -0 -1\r\nstd::cout << \"CC: \" << rotC.angle() << \" \" << rotC.axis().transpose() << std::endl; // 0.285223  0  0 -1\r\n```\r\n\r\nNote how the interpolated angle has value 0.285: it's taking the long way around, with the interpolation going through 0 instead of Pi/-Pi radians.","closed_by":{"login":"henningkayser","id":4572766,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1NzI3NjY=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4572766?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser","html_url":"https://github.com/henningkayser","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/henningkayser/events{/privacy}